data.content:
This is the **C# 14 span overload resolution** breaking change.

**What happened:** In C# 14, new built-in conversions make `Span<T>` / `ReadOnlySpan<T>` overloads applicable in more scenarios. When you write `array.Contains(num)`, the compiler now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. The `MemoryExtensions` method **cannot be represented in expression trees**, so it throws at runtime.

**How to fix — cast the array to `IEnumerable<T>`:**

```csharp
// Before (.NET 9) — binds to Enumerable.Contains, works in expression trees
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.Contains(num);

// After (.NET 10) — force Enumerable.Contains binding
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);
// or:
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Both approaches explicitly route to `Enumerable.Contains`, which is representable in expression trees. The same pattern applies to `.Reverse()` and other LINQ methods that now resolve to span-based extensions on arrays.
